          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 30-year-old woman develops fever and loin pain 5 days after an episode of cystitis. Which route of infection best explains the progression from bladder to renal involvement?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Haematogenous spread from a distant focus of infection</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Haematogenous spread is rare and typically involves Staphylococcus aureus bacteraemia causing renal abscess, or Mycobacterium tuberculosis reaching the kidney. It does not explain progression from pre-existing cystitis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Ascending route via the ureter from the bladder</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct! The ascending route accounts for over 95% of UTIs. Bacteria colonise the perineum, ascend the urethra to the bladder, and if untreated, continue up the ureters to cause pyelonephritis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Lymphatic drainage from adjacent pelvic organs</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Lymphatic spread to the urinary tract is not a recognised route for UTI pathogenesis. The ascending and haematogenous routes are the two established pathways.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Direct extension from a colonic diverticular abscess</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>While direct extension (e.g., vesicointestinal fistula) can introduce bacteria, this is uncommon and does not explain the typical cystitis-to-pyelonephritis progression seen here.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the routes of UTI. The ascending route (perineum → urethra → bladder → ureter → kidney) causes over 95% of UTIs and explains how cystitis progresses to pyelonephritis."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done! The ascending route is the dominant pathway for UTI, explaining why untreated lower UTIs can progress to upper tract involvement."
                }
              ],
